ic reactions that happen  for instance photolysis  and so in the end we  when we sample the environment ambient  water ambient air ambient soil what we  find is that we're sampling not just the  mix of chemicals that we started with  but a complex mixture of things that  have been sliced and diced by various  environmental reaction processes and so  that's where we're looking for impacts  and what we want to know is are these  compartments air water and soil are they  healthy for people who are exposed to  them or who consume them and  there's lots of different ways we could  assess this we could measure the  toxicity towards some  particular organism like an aquatic  invertebrate for example in a sediment  test  we might just assess whether they cause  particular biological changes like  mutagenicity or endocrine disruption  our goal in our lab in the red box is to  identify the compounds that are  responsible for that activity and  ultimately to try to connect them back  to the product choices that we've made  in order to give feedback to people who  make these products to tell them that  chemical a is a more safe or greener  alternative than chemical b  so that's the big picture of what we're  trying to do and i've chosen today to  talk about one specific project but i  wanted to provide some examples of other  things that we're doing in my lab at the  moment  so the next slide just tells us  provides two specific examples of other  projects that i won't be talking about  today the one on the left is our  superfund research program many of the  methods that are talked about today are  developed under that program um i'm the  deputy director of that program the  director bruce hammack and i have worked  together for a number of years and it's  really a collaboration between engineers  analytical chemists and people who do  mechanistic bioassays and mechanistic  biological impact studies and a lot of  this work and workflow that i'm going to  be talking about today grew out of that  effort  on the right-hand side i've just  highlighted a big project which we're  currently engaged in in which we're  measuring uh  drinking water samples treated drinking  water from eight different communities  in california at multiple time points  in each of those areas we have 15  households participating and we're doing  comprehensive non-target chemical  analysis of drinking water using the  kinds of techniques i'm going to be  talking about today those results are  still in process and in the center i've  just listed some other ongoing projects  that i don't really have time to talk  about today  so before we get to the specific project  i wanted to try to connect what i'm  going to be talking about to those of  you who might be watching this webinar i  know that a number of people might be  looking for for example business  opportunities related to  non-target chemical analysis because  it's not as widespread as targeted  analysis at the moment  and i wanted to draw your attention to  the way in which trends and policies  specifically in california but possibly  other places are colliding to make  non-target analysis and bioassays  kind of a hot topic so in california we  had a really large drought in the  2014-2015 period a lot of our aquifers  were drawn down  and there were wells that went dry and  so that caused the state to grapple with  this idea of sustainable groundwater  management what do we have to do to keep  our aquifer sustainable and what they  came up with was this uh sigma  sustainable groundwater management act  which says that you need to make sure  that your pumping of water from the  aquifer is sustainable and rather than  you know your two choices to do that are  to curtail use or to increase supply and  a lot of districts are trying to  increase their supply by recharging  their aquifers with a variety of water  sources including treated wastewater  agricultural return water storm water  etc  at the same time the state of california  another arm of the state of california  the state water resources control board  has adopted a water quality control  policy which says among other things  that you will test these waters that  might be used in an aquifer recharge  project for not only target compounds  like we might expect them to but also to  look for particular in vitro bioassay  results like estrogen receptor ag and  agonism and aero hydrocarbon receptor  agonism and so that's a water-based  example of the kinds of detective work  we're going to be talking about today  so this wildfire study grew out of the  the firestorm um in in 2017 in  california  and in the aftermath of those fires  there were a lot of questions about how  safe it was to go back in and  effectively sift through these ashes um  we know that  obviously a home fire is one thing but  an entire neighborhood burning down at  very high temperature in a wildfire is  another and it wasn't clear that when  you burn down the households that are  constructed from all these different  synthetic materials and containing  things like pesticides and solvents and  electrical insulation and so forth that  all of those compounds might not end up  in the ash and pose an extra risk to  people who are trying to  you know workers who are trying to  renovate the site or people who are  trying to reoccupy the site  so our goal was to understand whether  the ash from these residential wildfires  was intrinsically more harmful than the  ash that might be derived from a normal  forest-based wildfire  so to answer that question we designed a  study in which we took samples ranging  from the upper part of the map in the  center shows robert lewis stevenson  state park that serves as our  undeveloped reference site in this in  this case it was the northern boundary  of this particular piece of the fire and  the prevailing winds during this fire  were largely from the northeast to the  southwest so  angling down to the left in the map  there and so there's a transect of sites  at each general location we took three  samples so there's a total of five sites  four of them developed one of them  undeveloped at each site we have three  different samples and then we we  composited uh samples from these homes  and a couple of representative destroyed  homes are shown in photos on the left  and this fire destroyed over 5600  structures caused 22 deaths  and we were trying to understand whether  the sites in yellow  up at the top were really different in  their chemical composition and in their  biological impacts compared to the urban  or or residential sites  so what i want to do today is to show  you how we do this analysis  we have a unified workflow that applies  to both lc and gc high resolution mass  spectrometry data and then how we  connect that to the bioassay results  that we obtain and and then to just talk  a little bit about the chemical  composition difference and whether that  matters to what we're trying to say  something about which is toxicity  and as i've already mentioned we're  using these two instrumental platforms  both lc and gc  instruments and  we've got these total of 15 sites  12 of them with residences that were  destroyed  so to provide an overview of our  analytical method it's a fairly  straightforward and  not all that exciting  set of  sample processing so we take sieved ash  samples from these sites  we do a quick sonication extraction  sequenced  with hexane acetone and then acetone we  evaporate that filter it  we to do the lc side we do a solvent  exchange um on the gc side we add an  internal standard there's a series of  internal standards added to the to the  lc samples um the analysis is done on  two quadrupole time-of-flight  instruments the agilent 6530 lc and the  agilent 7200b  gc  the  lc samples are cleaned up  and and then run in both esi positive  and esi negative  and we run them initially in full scan  and then we go back and do targeted msms  on priori