our known target compounds that are  labeled these six compounds that are  labeled here  on the  x-axis are the ms-style area counts for  things that were automatically  identified without telling the program  that these were these compounds it  pulled these out and it identified them  and it came up with an area count that  is strongly linearly related to the  area count that we got from the  quantitative analysis program so you can  think of the y-axis as representing the  proper quantitation of these things and  you can think of the x-axis as as the um  you know  non-non-proper improper quantitation but  what it tells us is that when we have a  non-target feature which has a  particular area that we expect that area  count to be strongly related to the  quantity of chemical in the sample and  that's important because it tells us  that we can look for correlations  between the abundances in the non-target  data and the bioassay response so this  is uh hand waving to show that we can  use these area counts in a quantitative  way  so  just to summarize all of the results  that we got out of this  here i've shown a table which just  counts the number of compounds which we  pulled out of the data in various ways  and and what happened after we filtered  and what was related to bioassays  so  the three streams of data are the gc  electron ionization the liquid  chromatography with electrospray  positive and electrospray negative  what you can see is that there were  between hundreds and thousands of  aligned features that were pulled out of  that sample set we do a series of  quality control processes on those align  features we remove things with low  signal to noise we remove things that  are present in the blank at an  appreciable level we remove things that  aren't present in replicate injections  in the case of lc  and then we with that remaining set of  features which is still in the hundreds  to thousands  domain what we're looking for is  either a  correlation  between the response and and in this  case we're using uh spearman rank order  correlations between the bio essay  responses and the abundance of a feature  what we see just to focus in on the  sip1a1 line just so you can understand  what these numbers mean  we're looking at the gcei column sip1a1  and what that tells you is that there  were 33 features out of the 407 that  passed our filter that were positively  correlated with cytochrome p450 activity  at the 95 confidence level and there  were 11 features that were negatively  correlated with  the sip 1a1 response and at the 95  confidence level and then the last  number there the one that's labeled en  is stands for elastic net regression  elastic net regression is a technique  which is a is a reduction technique it's  like a lasso regression and it uh  you'll see it's pretty effective in  identifying out of  this kind of data where you have many  many descriptors and relatively few  samples it's a very good technique for  identifying significant things and so  there were 13 things that the elastic  net regression procedure picked out and  there's a significant overlap between  the correlated features and the elastic  net selected features so for each of our  six assays and each of our three  instrumental platforms you can see we  have varying numbers of positive and  negative correlations and the elastic  net regression in a number of cases  return none  no features zero features that were  related to that particular bio essay  endpoint so you'll see in general the  elastic net procedure is stingier in  assigning things and and later on you'll  see that it has a better performance in  terms of identifying things in in final  so out of those hundreds to thousands of  features if we look at what's actually  related to bioassays we've got a much  more manageable set between 76 and 276  compounds for the three different uh  platforms now you'll see that there's  many compounds that are  higher in the samples that 