ounds which is much larger  than the active compound set in talks  21.  and so when we look at that database the  ucd database  we get a much higher  fraction in well in all of the bins but  you can see that the performance goes up  but then the last thing we did was  compounds that weren't in talks 21 and  weren't in the uc davis database  were things that we could test ourselves  and so what we did is for the compounds  that we still didn't know whether they  were active or not but they were  correlated and we could get pure  standards we bought some pure standards  and we tested them  and  the estrogen receptor results here are  are similar but we just have a much less  uh significant database and so the  results look about the same as they did  for tox 21 only but we don't have this  additional ucd database or the target  tests for that piece so i can't provide  the last column of the table  so we went out and we bought five  compounds and we tested them on the  calex ahr  assay and what we found was that all  five of them had statistically  significant aero hydrocarbon receptor  agonist activity at different levels  here so you can see in the far right  column the prob value of the difference  between  the  assay result and the blank which is a  solvent blank  and in all but  two of these cases these  tests were statistically significant at  the 95 level and so  um what we confirmed was that this  technique this relationship of  non-target features to  to bioassay results that we obtain on  whole samples these samples weren't  fractionated in any way they were just  tested straight up on the assays we used  statistical procedures to fish things  out and as you see we had a very high  fraction of those compounds subsequently  proven to be active on the  receptor assay that we  fished them out with  and so this suggests that we can indeed  do what we're trying to do which is test  hole samples use statistical procedures  find the unknown compounds and identify  t