hem and confirm their presence in the  samples even when we have no prior  information about the compound's  biological activity  in question  so that's  been focused on the gc results and  frankly we have the best and most  comprehensive results for the gcei data  because and it's also probably a  function of the aero hydrocarbon  receptor test is the place we have the  best database and the compounds that are  in that  that are active in the aero hydrocarbon  receptor tend to be gc-able in a way  that the lc compounds aren't um  but i wanted to spend at least a couple  of minutes talking about how we do the  non-target  in silico annotation using msfinder  so we took our features which were  correlated with bioassay outcomes or  or were selected by the elastic not  regression procedure and we went back  and we tried to do targeted msms  experiments on all of them  we got a fairly low coverage of  successful targeted msms experiments  however  because  there's low abundance of some of these  things  and it's hard to get enough fragment  abundance the other thing that's  challenging is that many of these  molecular formula are pretty generic  they're they're cho or cnho type  compounds which  have many many possible structures and  not a lot of highly detailed  fragmentation  and therefore  they're not easy to nail down  but here i'm just providing one example  of how ms finder works we've taken a  particular feature out of our  alignment that we know to be  statistically important and related to  the bioassay results and we find that it  has an empirical formula of c23h22o  and the structure that we think it is is  shown down in the lower  right there  although this hasn't been confirmed but  this is the tentative match to that  particular molecular formula  given the insilico  program  so just to give you a couple specific  examples of things that are pulled out  from the ms finder  exercise these are two lc esi negative  on the left and two lc esi positive  compounds the red  spectral lines are the in silico  predicted fragments and the  black lines are the accurate mass  fragments measured in this  in this msms experiment that we  performed  between the inability to get a targeted  msns result for  these  samples  so there were many compounds we could  not obtain a targeted msms result for  and among the ones that we did get a  targeted msms there was often not enough  abundance to do very much with so  further efforts to concentrate select  purify would be helpful here and we're  working on  improving this set of the results  so where are we headed uh from here  first  we've proven that there are many things  in urban wildfire samples that were not  present in undeveloped  samples but based on the set of  bioassays we chose to apply here there's  not a systematic difference that that  those residential samples are more  bioactive and we also did not see any  systematic presence of the kinds of  compounds that you would see in a  typical environmental contaminants  database that is after this fire we  don't see a lot of residue of known  pesticides or known solvents  what we're seeing is  highly con  converted compounds that have been you  know subject to combustion  i think we can say that in this project  the gc  pathway yielded more success in  isolating and annotating compounds that  were related to the bio essay endpoints  and so for this purpose it was more  successful that  for other purposes it may not be  the same result obviously and then  there's the fundamental question about  whether we pick the right bioassays  there are many many different biological  endpoints that might be of concern and  it may be that the ones that we selected  are not the ones that we most need to  worry about and that's the challenge for  our toxicologists collaborators to  figure out what the right suite of  assays is to apply to these kinds of  samples  so i wanted to acknowledge the sources  of funding for this work  we had two  nih grants um that were focused on the  fires  the environmental health sciences core  center at uc davis provided some pilot  funding for wildfire analysis and a an  r21 grant from nih also provided funding  specifically to study this set of fires  so it was a rapid response grant  following the 2017 fires and as i  mentioned a lot of the methods that i  talked about here were developed under  the auspices of our superfund research  center  that's cited here  i didn't list them on the authors but as  you'll see in a minute they're an author  on the paper that we recently published  the west coast metabolomic center has  been really helpful  in helping my lab adapt and transform ms  dial and ms finder for these  kinds of applications and in particular  claibosis and oliver fien have  been really critical to this effort  and so now i want to close by just  making a plug  just two days ago our paper that covers  the work that i talked about here was  accepted for publication and  environmental science and technology  i can't tell you any more than that  because i don't even have the proofs for  the paper yet but you should look for it  shortly the citation is below  it'll be out  in a couple of days possibly by the by  the time this thing actually airs  because i'm recording it ahead of time  uh so i look forward to um addressing  any questions that you might have about  this um following the presentation and i  thanks  thank you for your attention and  interest in the work
 

